A language makes some facts explicit and leaves others for readers, compilers, or runtimes to recover. An implicit numeric conversion may save a cast at every call site, but it adds a rule to overload resolution and can conceal a loss of precision. A type annotation may make an interface easier to review without making local arithmetic any faster. These are different costs; counting the characters in example programs will not reveal either of them.

\section{State the Guarantees Before Choosing the Features}

In \emph{Hints on Programming Language Design}, C.~A.~R. Hoare puts readability ahead of ease of writing and uses ``security'' for the ability to detect programming errors rather than let them produce inexplicable machine-dependent effects. Michael L. Scott's \emph{Programming Language Pragmatics} adds an implementation question: who pays for a feature, including programs that do not use it? Together these questions suggest a way to frame a design decision: name the mistake to be caught, specify when it can be caught, and identify which programs bear the cost.

For example, a language for inspecting weather-station logs cannot statically prove that every future input record contains a valid temperature. It can statically require that a program distinguish a temperature from a failed conversion. Detecting the bad input is then a dynamic obligation; handling the distinction is a static one. Write this division into the type signatures and the evaluation rules, not merely a manual. A compiler and an interpreter can otherwise both accept the same file while disagreeing about where a conversion failure occurs.

\section{Worked Example: Three Read Operations}

Suppose the input record has a site identifier and a temperature field. Compare three signatures, with the same underlying lexical decoder:

\begin{verbatim}
readTemp(row)                 -> Temperature or raises BadField
readTemp(row)                 -> Result<Temperature, FieldError>
readTempOr(row, fallback)      -> Temperature
\end{verbatim}

The first policy supports a short straight-line program, with a handler around the whole job. It may also discard a day's computation because of one bad field. The second policy lets a per-row consumer choose whether to skip, quarantine, or stop. A type checker can require the program to unwrap the result before adding the temperature; it cannot prove the chosen recovery policy sensible. The third policy is defensible in a display that deliberately substitutes a tagged placeholder, but a bare fallback of zero makes an invalid record indistinguishable from a measured zero in a later average.

Run the three designs against records for the same site with temperatures \texttt{10}, \texttt{?}, and \texttt{20}. Under an outer fail-fast handler, no average is published. Under explicit recovery by skipping, the average is 15 and the error count is one. Under untagged defaulting the average is 10 and the accepted-reading count is three. The observable difference is not the spelling of a call; it is what the caller learns about the input and can publish without lying.

Kathleen Fisher, Yitzhak Mandelbaum, and David Walker offer a more structured alternative in \emph{The Next 700 Data Description Languages}: interpret a data description both as a representation type and as a parser, and return information about physical and semantic errors. For the log language, choose an invariant inspired by that separation: every presented row receives exactly one disposition (accepted or rejected), and its contribution to the average is present if and only if it was accepted. A test should check the disposition as well as the number, including when the only well-formed measurement is zero.

\section{Compare Designs on the Same Workload}

Evaluate the alternatives against at least two jobs: a one-shot import that must reject a corrupt file and an interactive preview that must continue after a corrupt line. Compare the number of decisions required in user code, the source location in diagnostics, and runtime cost in success-heavy and error-heavy inputs. Keep the decoder and data fixed so that a faster parsing algorithm does not get credited to a change in error semantics. Include a separately compiled client: making failure visible only after whole-program analysis is a poor contract for a public interface.

Disagreement across workloads is a result, not a defect in the comparison. A sensible language may expose both fail-fast import and explicit recovery, while giving silent substitution a distinctive name and requiring an argument. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/16-effect-aware-ir.md}{dossier's Effect-Aware IR chapter} asks the corresponding compiler question: once an operation can fail, what dependencies must a transformation preserve? Moving a potentially failing conversion ahead of a conditional that guards it, for example, can change a skipped record into a job-ending exception.
